#
# prima-materia — the Chinese medical canon
#
# Order of the file: the tradition and the route that had to be decided before
# its first node, then the needling and the two sets of prohibitions that hang
# on it, then the disagreement the transmitted text carries about its own
# death intervals, then the two signs by which the operation is said to have
# taken and the one condition under which the source says it cannot take at
# all, then the one capacity in this corpus that comes with a test procedure,
# and last the regimen and the graded persons of the opening chapter.
#
# The tradition instance was registered in traditions/registry-east-asian.ttl
# and is moved here now that passages have been read.
#
# Opened from the independence entrance of SPEC §14: it stood at
# pm:contactRouteAbsent, which is the value that makes a witness countable.
# The reading corrected the entrance rather than confirming it; the reason is
# on the instance.
#

pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Chinese medical canon"@en, "Chinesischer medizinischer Kanon"@de ;
    skos:definition "The canonical Chinese transmission of needling, of the qi and the vessels, and of the spirits held to reside in the depots, set out as dialogues between the Yellow Lord and his interlocutors and carrying, alongside the doctrine, its own prohibitions, its own intervals to death and its own signs by which an operation is said to have taken."@en ;
    skos:note "Chosen from the registry on the independence entrance, and the reading corrected that entrance. The contact route is entered as documented and no longer as absent, and the route runs to a transmission this graph already holds. The received text is not the Han-period text but the recension Wang Bing completed in seven hundred and sixty-two as the editors of ten sixty-seven passed it on, and that recension is constituted by its commentary: the commentary printed with the opening chapter quotes the Laozi twice and names the Heshanggong commentary on it, and the commentary elsewhere in the same juan quotes the Zhuangzi. The Laozi and the Zhuangzi are the corpus the Daoist file in this graph rests part of its material on. An agreement between this file and that one is therefore not two witnesses at the level of the transmitted text, whatever may hold of the stratum beneath it."@en ;
    skos:note "The correction is narrower than the value can express, and the difference matters for any convergence built on this file. What is documented is a route at the point where the corpus was fixed in the form read, in the eighth century and again in the eleventh. Whether the first-century material that the compilation rests on had such a route is a separate question, and it is not decided here; the predicate is single-valued and cannot hold both answers. The corresponding question for the Indian and the Greek material was searched and stands as its own issue."@en ;
    skos:note "Read at the wording, in the Chinese, on 2026-09-10, in addition to the passages of the first run: Suwen 13, 19, 20, 23, 25 and 64; Lingshu 10, 32, 60, 61, 70 and 78; the Lingshu chapter on examining the forearm, in a second edition for the reason given at the node that rests on it; and Nanjing 20 and 61 with the commentary apparatus printed with them. The Nanjing enters this file with those two places and had until then been searched by term only."@en ;
    skos:note "Read in the first run at the wording, in the Chinese: Suwen 1, 14, 16, 26, 52 and 54, and Lingshu 8, 9 and 73, each in the juan named in the citations below. The Song editors' collation notes printed with Suwen 16 and Suwen 52 were read with them and carry the dispute node in this file. The parallel in the Zhenjiu jiayi jing was opened for the same passage and is cited as the second witness there. The remainder of both classics, and the whole of the Nanjing, were searched by term and not read continuously; what that turned up and did not verify stands in the issues named in the harvest note, not here."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition note. Both classics were read in the Sibu congkan facsimiles, which is the received text and not a critical edition: for the Suwen the Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen with Wang Bing's commentary and the Song collation notes, for the Lingshu the Huangdi suwen lingshu jing. The digitisation used names the collection and not the printing within it, and the citations say so by naming the collection. Where a reading is contested the contest is entered as a node and not smoothed into the citation."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Huangdi neijing (Suwen and Lingshu); Nanjing; Huangfu Mi, Zhenjiu jiayi jing; Yang Shangshan, Huangdi neijing taisu" ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 52 (Ci jin lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 14" .

#
# The needling and its prohibitions
#

pmp:Cizhen a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Needling"@en, "Nadelung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The insertion of a needle at a named place on the body to a stated depth, held to move the qi of a depot or a vessel either towards fullness or towards emptiness. The corpus treats it throughout as an operation that can go wrong, and states the going wrong in the same register as the going right."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 52 (Ci jin lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 14" .

pmc:CizhongWuzang a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Striking a depot: the interval and the sign"@en, "Der Treffer ins Depot: die Frist und das Zeichen"@de ;
    skos:definition "A needle driven into one of the depots is said to kill after a stated interval, and each interval is paired with a bodily sign by which the strike is to be recognised. The heart: death in one day, the movement belching. The liver: five days, speech. The kidney: six days, sneezing. The lung: three days, coughing. The spleen: ten days, swallowing. The gall bladder: a day and a half, vomiting. The same chapter continues with strikes that maim rather than kill and names the outcome for each: into the brain through the occipital opening, death on the spot; the vessel in the face, blindness; the marrow between the vertebrae, a bent back; the knee cap, lameness; the arm's great yin vessel, immediate death from loss of blood."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because of the form and not the content. This is a claim that names an outcome it excludes: a person struck in the heart who is alive on the third day is not covered by any weaker reading of the sentence, and the sign attached to each interval says what the strike is supposed to look like while the interval runs. Very little in this graph is stated that way."@en ;
    skos:note "The intervals are not stable across the witnesses, and the transmitted text says so about itself. That is held in the dispute node below rather than in this definition, which gives the numbers as the recension read prints them."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Cizhen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 52 (Ci jin lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 14" .

pmc:WucijinQizhuang a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The seven states in which one does not needle"@en, "Die sieben Zustände, in denen nicht genadelt wird"@de ;
    skos:definition "Seven conditions of the person are named in which the needle is not to be used, and the first two carry their consequence with them: do not needle one greatly drunk, it makes the qi disordered; do not needle one greatly angered, it makes the qi go against its course; do not needle one greatly exhausted, nor one newly filled with food, nor one greatly hungry, nor one greatly thirsty, nor one greatly startled."@en ;
    skos:note "The consequence is stated for the first two and withheld for the remaining five, which the file keeps as it stands rather than extending the pattern. Where a later chapter of the same corpus states consequences for the whole list, that is a second and differently worded passage and it is entered separately."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Cizhen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 52 (Ci jin lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 14" .

pmc:ShierjinZhongshi a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The twelve prohibitions, and what is said to follow from breaking them"@en, "Die zwölf Verbote und was ihr Bruch bewirken soll"@de ;
    skos:definition "The needling chapter of the Lingshu states the same prohibitions as a two-sided rule and adds a state the other list does not have. Newly after intercourse do not needle, and newly needled do not lie together; already drunk do not needle, and already needled do not get drunk; newly angered, newly exhausted, already filled, already hungry, already thirsty, each in the same double form. On great fright and great fear the rule is not a prohibition at all but a condition: settle the qi first, then needle. Two further clauses set waiting times by the manner of arrival, the time of a meal for one who came by cart and the time of walking ten li for one who came on foot. The chapter then names what breaking the twelve does: the vessels are disordered and the qi scattered, a yang illness enters the yin and a yin illness comes out as yang, the evil qi is generated afresh, and the text calls it hewing at the body, wasting the brain marrow and losing the qi."@en ;
    skos:note "The relation to the Suwen list is not similarity but disagreement, and the Song collation note printed with the Suwen chapter says so in as many words: it states that the seven clauses there and the Lingshu clauses go in and out of one another, and it prints the Lingshu wording beside them so that a reader can see it. The two differ on fright, which one forbids and the other admits after a delay, and on intercourse, which only the Lingshu has."@en ;
    skos:note "This is also the passage in this corpus that comes closest to a stated failure condition for the operator rather than the patient. It says what the transgression produces, in the body, and names the direction of the change."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Cizhen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 9 (Zhong shi), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 2" .

#
# The corpus disagreeing with itself about its own intervals
#

pmc:CizhongIntervalDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "How long after a depot is struck death is said to follow"@en, "Wie lange nach dem Treffer ins Depot der Tod eintreten soll"@de ;
    skos:definition "Four places in the same canon give differing intervals for the same strike, and a further witness gives another set. The needling-prohibition chapter has the kidney at six days, the lung at three and the spleen at ten. The chapter on the essentials of examination has the heart dying within one circuit rather than in one day, the spleen at five days, the kidney at seven, the lung at five, and adds a strike through the diaphragm after which the patient dies within the year however well he seems to recover. The chapter on needling with and against the four seasons repeats the whole list a third time, agreeing with the needling-prohibition chapter at heart, liver, lung and spleen, giving the kidney's movement sign as sneezing and yawning where the other has sneezing alone, omitting the gall bladder, and adding a summary clause the others do not have: that whoever in needling injures a man's five depots kills him for certain, and that his death is to be known from the movement sign of the depot concerned. The Zhenjiu jiayi jing has the kidney at three days, the spleen at fifteen, and gives the liver's sign as a different character from the one the received Suwen prints. The Song editors record in their collation notes that the older recension of Quan Yuanqi agreed with the Jiayi jing on both numbers, and that Wang Bing changed the liver's sign."@en ;
    skos:note "The dispute is entered because the transmitted text states it about itself and states a verdict on it. The collation note printed with the chapter on the essentials of examination says that these three discourses are all the words of Qi Bo and that their differing is an error of transmission. That is one party's account of the divergence and not a neutral finding, and the graph keeps it as such."@en ;
    skos:note "The commentators do not treat the numbers as observations. The note beside the spleen argues from ten being the completion number of earth, and other commentators have wanted to correct liver and lung to the generation numbers of wood and metal. A number defended from a numerology is not thereby false, but it is not evidence of a counted interval either, and a protocol on this claim would have to begin there."@en ;
    skos:note "The consequence for this graph is narrower than the dispute. Nothing here says that the intervals are wrong; what it says is that a citation naming one chapter conceals that the same canon carries three answers, and that the earliest reachable witness disagrees with the received text at two of the six places."@en ;
    skos:note "The third Suwen chapter was added on 2026-09-10 after being read at the wording, and it changes the balance rather than adding a fourth set of numbers. Two of the three Suwen chapters now agree on five of the six intervals, so the divergence inside the received text is narrower than the earlier state of this node suggested; what remains is the chapter on the essentials of examination against the other two, and the Jiayi jing against all three. The clause that whoever injures the five depots kills for certain stands only in the added chapter, and it is the sentence that turns a list of intervals into a claim that admits no surviving case."@en ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:CizhongWuzang ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 52 (Ci jin lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 14", "Huangdi neijing suwen 16 (Zhen yao jing zhong lun), with the Xin jiao zheng collation notes, in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 4", "Huangdi neijing suwen 64 (Si shi ci ni cong lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 18", "Huangfu Mi, Zhenjiu jiayi jing, juan 5 (Zhen jiu jin ji), in the Wenyuange Siku quanshu text" .

#
# What is said to show that it took, and what is said to make it impossible
#

pmc:QizhiYouxiao a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The arrival of the qi, and the sign that is refused"@en, "Das Ankommen des Qi und das zurückgewiesene Zeichen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Lingshu states what it means for the qi to have arrived and to have had effect, and states it as a rule over the vessel and not over the patient. Where the operation drained, the vessel is to be as large as before but no longer hard; if it is hard as before, then although the patient says it is as it was, the illness has not gone. Where the operation supplemented, the vessel is to be as large as before and harder; if it is as before and not hard, then although the patient says he feels relieved, the illness has not gone. The chapter closes the rule from the other side: even where the pain does not follow the needle, the illness must be in retreat."@en ;
    skos:note "The reason for entering this rather than the many other success signs in the corpus. A success sign told to the practised beforehand is worth little, because he will report it; this passage names such a report and refuses it twice over, in the same sentence in which it gives the criterion that replaces it. The corpus therefore holds a stated distinction between what a patient says and what counts as the operation having taken, and the distinction runs against the interest of the operator."@en ;
    skos:note "What the passage does not supply is a way of reading the vessel that two examiners would read alike. The criterion is hardness under the finger, which is why this is entered as a sign the tradition names and not as a procedure the graph can use."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 9 (Zhong shi), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 2" .

pmc:ZhenxiaReHan a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Heat and cold under the needle"@en, "Wärme und Kälte unter der Nadel"@de ;
    skos:definition "A second sign, stated in the chapter that explains the nine needles: where emptiness is to be filled, it is warm under the needle, for the qi becomes full and then it is warm; where fullness is to be drained, it is cold under the needle, for the qi becomes empty and then it is cold. The same chapter makes the warmth the criterion for withdrawal, saying that in needling an emptiness one waits for the fullness, and that when the yang qi has arrived in force and it is warm under the needle, then the needle is taken out."@en ;
    skos:note "Held apart from the vessel criterion above, and the difference is the point. This one is felt by the operator at the needle and is not stated to be checkable by anybody else; the other is a rule over a finding that at least two people can take. A tradition that carries both is a tradition that has not settled which of them decides, and the file does not settle it either."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 54 (Zhen jie pian), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 14" .

pmc:Jingshen a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The essence-spirit and the intent of the sick person"@en, "Essenzgeist und Absicht des Kranken"@de ;
    skos:definition "The condition of the patient on which the corpus makes the whole operation depend: the essence and the spirit, and with them the intent, held to be present or absent and to be won or lost. The Lingshu instructs the needler to observe the bearing of the sick person in order to know whether essence, spirit, hun and po are present or gone; where these five are damaged, it says, the needle cannot treat him."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 8 (Ben shen), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 2" .

pmc:Shenbushi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The spirit does not lend itself"@en, "Der Geist gibt sich nicht her"@de ;
    skos:definition "The term the corpus gives to the case in which the body is worn out and the blood exhausted and the treatment establishes nothing. Asked why that happens, the answer is that the spirit does not lend itself. Needle and stone, the passage continues, are a way; where the essence-spirit does not go forward and the intent is not ordered, the illness cannot be cured; and where the essence is ruined and the spirit gone, the constructive and defensive qi can no longer be gathered back, because desire is without end and grief without cease."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the failure condition of this corpus that is hardest to test and most worth recording, because it is located in the patient and not in the operation. Any outcome of a treatment can be assigned to it after the fact, and the passage supplies no mark by which its presence could be established beforehand. Recorded as the tradition's own account of why a correct procedure fails, and marked here as the shape of claim that absorbs every outcome."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 14 (Tang ye lao li lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 4" .

pmc:CizhenPresupposesJingshen a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Needling presupposes that the spirit is present"@en, "Die Nadelung setzt voraus, dass der Geist da ist"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:Cizhen ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:Jingshen ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Two passages state it and both state a consequence rather than a degree. Where the essence-spirit does not go forward and the intent is not ordered, the illness cannot be cured, and the body worn out and the blood exhausted the treatment establishes nothing. Where the five, essence, spirit, hun, po and the intent, are damaged, the needle cannot treat him at all."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 14 (Tang ye lao li lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 4", "Huangdi neijing lingshu 8 (Ben shen), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 2" .

#
# The one capacity in this corpus that comes with a test
#

pmc:Shoudu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The poisonous hand"@en, "Die giftige Hand"@de ;
    skos:definition "A property of a person's hands, named in the chapter that assigns offices according to fitness: some have bitter nails and poisonous hands and in doing things are apt to injure, and such a person is to be set to pressing on accumulations and holding down numbness. The chapter opposes it to the sweet hand. It stands in a list beside the assignment of the well-jointed and supple-sinewed to the leading and pulling of the qi, and of the sharp-tongued and slighting to the spitting on abscesses and the incanting of illnesses."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim is that the difference is a property of the person and not of what he does, since the chapter assigns offices by it and says that where the person is not the right one the work does not succeed and his teacher goes without a name."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 73 (Guan neng), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 11" .

pmp:AnguiShi a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The pressing of the turtle"@en, "Die Probe an der Schildkröte"@de ;
    skos:definition "The procedure the chapter gives for deciding whether a person has the poisonous hand. A turtle is placed under a vessel and the person presses on the vessel from above. If his hand is poisonous, the turtle is dead in fifty days; if his hand is sweet, it lives on as before."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as a practice in its own right because of what it is: a discrimination procedure, stated by the tradition, over a claim about a person, with a fixed term and two outcomes that are not the same. Nothing else read in this corpus in this run has that shape, and the reason for opening the corpus from the testability side was the expectation of finding it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 73 (Guan neng), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 11" .

#
# The regimen and the graded persons of the opening chapter
#

pmp:ZhidaoYangsheng a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The regimen of those who knew the way"@en, "Das Regimen derer, die den Weg kannten"@de ;
    skos:definition "The conduct the opening chapter ascribes to the people of high antiquity who knew the way: they took the yin and the yang as their model and were in accord with the arts and the numbers, they were measured in food and drink and regular in rising and resting, and they did not work recklessly. The chapter states the counter-case in the same breath, of the people of the present who take wine for water, take the reckless for the regular, enter the bedchamber drunk, exhaust their essence in desire and are without measure in rising and resting."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 1 (Shang gu tian zhen lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 1" .

pmc:DubaisuiNaiqu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The hundred years fully lived out"@en, "Die vollständig gelebten hundert Jahre"@de ;
    skos:definition "The outcome the same chapter attaches to that conduct: form and spirit together, the heaven-given years carried to their end, a hundred years passed through and then departure. Against it stands the stated outcome of the counter-case, decline at half a hundred."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as a capacity claim and not as a piece of cosmology because the chapter states both arms and states them in the same terms. It is nonetheless not a claim any protocol reaches from here: the antecedent is a manner of living over a lifetime and the corpus supplies no way of establishing that it was kept."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 1 (Shang gu tian zhen lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 1" .

pmc:YangshengYieldsDubaisui a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The regimen yields the full span"@en, "Das Regimen bringt die volle Spanne hervor"@de ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ZhidaoYangsheng ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:DubaisuiNaiqu ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 1 (Shang gu tian zhen lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 1" .

pmc:ZhenrenZhirenShengrenXianren a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The four graded persons of the first chapter"@en, "Die vier gestuften Personen des ersten Kapitels"@de ;
    skos:definition "Four grades, each with a stated reach and each ascribed to a conduct rather than to a birth. The true persons of high antiquity lifted and held heaven and earth, grasped the yin and the yang, breathed the essence-qi, stood alone and guarded the spirit, and their flesh was as one, so that their years wore out heaven and earth and had no ending time. Next the perfected persons, whose virtue was whole, who were in accord with yin and yang and in tune with the four seasons, who left the age and departed from custom, who accumulated the essence and made the spirit whole, who roamed between heaven and earth and whose seeing and hearing went beyond the eight reaches. Next the sages, who dwelt in the harmony of heaven and earth and followed the pattern of the eight winds, whose bodily form was not worn out and whose essence and spirit were not scattered, and who likewise could be counted in hundreds. Last the worthies, who took heaven and earth for their rule and modelled themselves on sun and moon, and who could lengthen their years but had a limit."@en ;
    skos:note "The two upper grades carry the claims that make this a capacity list rather than a moral one: a lifespan coextensive with heaven and earth and without end, and a seeing and hearing that reaches beyond the eight quarters. The two lower grades carry no such claim and are bounded in the text itself, the third at a count in hundreds and the fourth explicitly at a limit."@en ;
    skos:note "Nothing in the chapter says that the two upper grades were observed, and nothing says how one would tell a perfected person from a worthy. The grades are entered as what the corpus claims and not as a scale the graph can place anyone on."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 1 (Shang gu tian zhen lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 1" .

pmc:Dujian a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Seeing alone where all are looking"@en, "Allein sehen, wo alle hinsehen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The corpus is asked what it means by spirit and answers with a description of a perception that precedes the senses. The ear does not hear; the eye is bright and the heart opens and the intent goes first; he understands alone and clearly and the mouth cannot say it; all look and he alone sees; it is as though dark, and he alone is bright, as when wind blows the clouds away. The chapter sets this against the corresponding account of form, where what is sought is found by asking after the illness and searching it in the vessels."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because it is the clearest statement in this corpus that a capacity of the practitioner is meant and not a technique. It is also the least examinable: the passage says in the same breath that the mouth cannot say it, so nothing in the description could be set against an outcome."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 26 (Ba zheng shen ming lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 8" .

pmc:SituatingDujianAnchoring a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "Seeing alone is placed at withdrawn absorption"@en, "Das Alleinsehen wird bei abgezogener Vertiefung verortet"@de ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:Dujian ;
    pm:axisValue pm:withdrawnAbsorption ;
    pm:situationGround "The passage names the withdrawal of the ordinary channel itself and does not leave it to be inferred: the ear does not hear, and where all look he alone sees. The practitioner is awake and at work on a patient throughout, so what is set aside is the reliance on what the senses report and not waking itself. That is the criterion this coordinate names."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 26 (Ba zheng shen ming lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 8" .

#
# The prohibitions bound to a date, and the measure
#

pmc:Wujin a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The five prohibitions bound to the day stem"@en, "Die fünf Verbote nach dem Tagesstamm"@de ;
    skos:definition "A second system of prohibitions, independent of the one in Suwen 52 and bound not to a state of the person but to the day. On jia and yi days, when they mount themselves, do not needle the head and do not open the darkness at the inner ear; on bing and ding days do not shake the dust at shoulder, throat and lianquan; on wu and ji days, when they mount the four seasons, do not needle the belly and do not remove the claw to drain the water; on geng and xin days do not needle the joints at thigh and knee; on ren and gui days do not needle foot and shin. The chapter calls these the five prohibitions and opens by defining a prohibition as forbidding what may not be needled."@en ;
    skos:note "The reason for entering this rather than the many other prohibitions of the corpus. Every other prohibition read here attaches to something about the person before the needle — drunk, angry, exhausted, newly fed, the spirit not present. This one attaches to the date and to nothing else, and the date is fixed by a calendar that stands before the case and outside the operator's judgement. That makes it the one prohibition in this corpus a third party could check without examining anybody."@en ;
    skos:note "What it does not carry is a consequence. The chapter names the days and the places and stops; it does not say what happens to one needled at the head on a jia day. The named operations it forbids are the ones the corpus elsewhere calls the five sections, so the prohibition is stated over a technical vocabulary and not over the body's parts alone."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Cizhen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 61 (Wu jin), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 9" .

pmc:Wuduo a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The five deprivations, in which nothing may be drained"@en, "Die fünf Beraubungen, in denen nicht abgeleitet werden darf"@de ;
    skos:definition "Five states of prior loss in which the draining operation is forbidden outright: the flesh already lost; after a great loss of blood; after a great sweating; after a great purging; and after childbirth or a great flow of blood. The chapter closes the list with the sentence that in all of these one may not drain."@en ;
    skos:note "Held apart from the seven states of Suwen 52 and the twelve of Lingshu 9 because the criterion is different. Those forbid needling in a condition of the moment, drunkenness or anger or hunger; these forbid one half of the operation after a loss that has already happened, and the prohibition is on draining and not on needling as such. A transmission that separates the two halves of its own operation this way is saying something about the operation."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Cizhen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 61 (Wu jin), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 9" .

pmc:Wuguo a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "In supplementing and draining, do not exceed the measure"@en, "Beim Füllen und Ableiten das Maß nicht überschreiten"@de ;
    skos:definition "The whole of what the chapter says about the five excesses. Asked what the five excesses of needling are, the answer is one clause: in supplementing and in draining, do not go beyond their measure."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the corpus's general rule of measure against its own operation, and it is remarkable for how little it carries. It names no quantity, no sign by which the measure would be recognised, and no consequence of exceeding it, while the same chapter states the five prohibitions and the five deprivations with places and states attached. A rule of measure with nothing to measure by is entered as what the corpus says and not as a procedure."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Cizhen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 61 (Wu jin), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 9" .

pmc:WulaoSuoshang a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The five exhaustions and what each injures"@en, "Die fünf Erschöpfungen und was jede schädigt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The corpus's one general statement that a thing done too long injures, with the injury named for each: looking long injures the blood, lying long injures the qi, sitting long injures the flesh, standing long injures the bone, walking long injures the sinews. The Suwen closes the list by naming it the five injuries of exhaustion; the Lingshu prints the same five clauses and calls them the illnesses of the five long exhaustions."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered as the answer this corpus gives to a question the graph asks of many transmissions: whether the excess of a practice is said to harm and whether a measure is prescribed. The answer here is general in form and has a stated consequence for each case, which is more than most. What it does not say is that the excess of a prescribed practice harms; the five things it names are ordinary postures and not techniques, and the corpus's rule about its own operation is the bare clause about measure entered beside this."@en ;
    skos:note "The doublet is not a variant. Both classics carry the same five clauses with the same organs and the same order, one under the heading of the five exhaustions and one in the chapter on the nine needles; the wording of the closing formula differs and the list does not."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ZhidaoYangsheng ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 23 (Xuan ming wu qi), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 7", "Huangdi neijing lingshu 78 (Jiu zhen lun), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 12" .

#
# The needle as an instrument of killing, and the counted dose
#

pmc:ShashengBuqisi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "It can kill the living and cannot raise the dead"@en, "Sie kann Lebende töten und keine Toten erwecken"@de ;
    skos:definition "The chapter sets the small needle beside the five weapons and says that nothing is greater than the needle except them, that the five weapons are the equipment of death and not the instruments of life. Asked directly whether an instrument that can kill the living and cannot raise the dead might be turned round, the answer is that it cannot: it can kill the living and cannot raise the dead. The chapter compares it to a blade, which can kill a man, and to wine, which makes him drunk, and says that this can be known without examining anybody."@en ;
    skos:note "The asymmetry is stated by the corpus about its own instrument and is not a modern reading of it. It matters for this graph because the same corpus states the killing side with intervals and signs and the healing side with a criterion the operator feels at the needle. The transmission is more exact about what it can destroy than about what it can accomplish, and it says so."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 60 (Yu ban), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 9" .

pmc:WuliErshiwu a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Five times five at the wuli, and the transports are drained"@en, "Fünfmal fünf am Wuli, und die Zuflüsse sind erschöpft"@de ;
    skos:definition "The one counted threshold in this corpus. Asked whether there is a number to it, the answer is that one meets it at the wuli and stops midway; five arrivals and that is all; five goings and the qi of a depot is exhausted; therefore five fives, twenty-five, and the transports are drained. The chapter calls this robbing a man of his heaven-given qi and denies that it is a cutting short of his allotted span. It adds the two cases: he who needles peering in from the gate, the man dies in the house; he who enters the gate and needles, he dies in the hall. The chapter ends by ordering the matter written on jade tablets and handed to later ages as a prohibition of needling, so that the people dare not transgress it."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because of its shape and not its plausibility. Every other lethal claim in this corpus attaches to a place — a depot, a vessel, a region — and this one attaches to a number of repetitions at a single named point. A dose is a different kind of claim from a location: it can be exceeded by degrees, and the transmission that states one has said what an operator would have to count."@en ;
    skos:note "The two gate clauses are the part that resists any reading as observation. They make the interval to death depend on where the operator was standing, which no arrangement of the body could produce, and they stand in the same passage as the count. What the graph holds here is therefore one claim with two halves of very different kinds, and it does not separate them, because the source does not."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Cizhen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 60 (Yu ban), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 9" .

pmc:WuniYuban a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The two sets of five counter-flows, each with its term"@en, "Die zwei Fünfergruppen der Widersetzlichkeit, jede mit ihrer Frist"@de ;
    skos:definition "Three lists of five in one chapter. The first is for a wound gone to pus: the white of the eye blue and the black of the eye small; vomiting what is given by mouth; pain in the belly with great thirst; the shoulder and neck not free; the voice hoarse and the colour gone. Except for these five, the case runs with the grain. The second set of five carries a term: a distended belly with a hot body and a large pulse; the belly rumbling and full with the four limbs cold and purging, and the pulse large; nosebleed that will not stop, and a large pulse; coughing with blood in the urine, the flesh gone, and a small taut pulse; coughing, the flesh gone, the body hot, and a small quick pulse. Of these the chapter says that such a one dies within fifteen days. The third set carries a shorter term: a greatly distended belly with the four extremities cold, the flesh gone and severe purging; a distended belly with blood in the stool and a large pulse that intermits; coughing with blood in the urine, the flesh inwardly gone, and a beating pulse; vomiting blood with a full chest drawing to the back, and a small quick pulse; coughing and vomiting with a distended belly and undigested stools, and the pulse cut off. Of these it says that such a one dies within a single hour. It adds that a workman who does not examine for these and needles anyway is performing a treatment against the grain, and elsewhere in the chapter that once the pus has formed, ten die and one lives."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the most examinable prognostic material read in this corpus, and its form is why. Each item is a conjunction of findings that a second examiner could be asked to confirm, the two terms are short enough to be observed out, and the two terms are different, so the lists are not a single undivided claim about the gravely ill. Against that stands the pulse, which is the finding two examiners are least likely to take alike, and which appears in nine of the ten."@en ;
    skos:note "The chapter states no exception and names no case in which such a patient lives. That is what distinguishes it from the year-prognosis of the red vessels, which names the finding under which it predicts nothing at all."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 60 (Yu ban), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 9" .

#
# Death read from a calendar and from an hour
#

pmc:JingmaiQijue a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The severing of a channel's qi, its sign and its stem day"@en, "Das Abreißen des Leitbahn-Qi, sein Zeichen und sein Stammtag"@de ;
    skos:definition "For each of five yin channels a sign of the qi having been severed, a part of the body said to die first, and a pair of stem days on which the case grows grave and on which death follows, each with a reason from the sequence in which the phases overcome one another. Hand taiyin: skin and hair scorched, the hair breaking, the hair dies first; grave at bing, dead at ding, fire overcoming metal. Hand shaoyin: the vessel not passing, the face black as lacquered kindling, the blood dies first; grave at ren, dead at gui, water over fire. Foot taiyin: the flesh soft, the tongue withered, the lips turned back, the flesh dies first; grave at jia, dead at yi, wood over earth. Foot shaoyin: the bone dried, the teeth grown long and foul, the hair without lustre, the bone dies first; grave at wu, dead at ji, earth over water. Foot jueyin: the sinews taut, the lips blue and the tongue curled and the testicles drawn up, the sinews die first; grave at geng, dead at xin, metal over wood. Two collective cases follow: all five yin severed together, the eye connection turns and the eyes roll, the intent dies first, and death is at most a day and a half away; the six yang severed, yin and yang part, the interstices open and the sweat of dying comes out, and one reckoned in the morning dies in the evening, one reckoned in the evening dies in the morning."@en ;
    skos:note "The interest of this passage for the graph is that it shows the corpus deriving a term rather than reporting one. The day is not a number of days from the observation; it is a named day of the sexagenary cycle, and the text gives the derivation openly in each case, from the phase that overcomes the phase of the channel. Whatever the intervals of Suwen 52 are, these are not counted intervals, and the chapter does not pretend otherwise."@en ;
    skos:note "The two collective cases are of the other kind. A day and a half, and the morning-for-evening rule, are stated as durations from the finding, with no calendar in them. One chapter therefore carries both forms, which is the comparison a protocol on the intervals of this corpus would have to begin from."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 10 (Jing mai), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 3" .

pmc:JiuhouSishi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The hour of death read from the pulse and the season it stands for"@en, "Die Todesstunde, aus dem Puls und seiner Jahreszeit gelesen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Where all nine positions of the pulse are sunken, fine and cut off, that is yin and rules winter, so death comes at midnight; where they are full, restless and hurried, that is yang and rules summer, so death comes at noon. Then by illness: one with a cold-and-heat illness dies at first light; heat within and heat illness, at noon; wind illness, at evening; water illness, at midnight; and where the pulse is now sparse now frequent, now slow now quick, death comes on the day that rides the four seasons. The chapter adds two rules that cut across the scheme: where the flesh is already gone, the patient dies although the nine positions are in order; and where the seven signs appear but the nine positions all run with the grain, he does not die."@en ;
    skos:note "The derivation is stated and is the point. The hour is not observed and then recorded; it is read off a correspondence, pulse to yin or yang, yin or yang to a season, season to an hour of the day. The chapter says so in the order of its own clauses, which is why this is entered beside the stem days of Lingshu 10 rather than beside the day counts of Suwen 52."@en ;
    skos:note "The second of the two crossing rules is the more interesting and the harder to use. It says that the seven signs may be present and the patient not die, provided the nine positions run with the grain, and the chapter immediately qualifies even that by naming illnesses that resemble the seven signs and are not them. A prognosis with an exception whose scope is settled by the prognosticator is what the graph holds here."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 20 (San bu jiu hou lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 6" .

#
# Prognosis with a stated term and, in one case, a stated non-prediction
#

pmc:Zhenzangmai a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The bare depot pulses and the graded terms"@en, "Die Pulse der bloßen Depots und die gestuften Fristen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Five pulses named as the depot appearing bare, each given as a thing felt under the finger with a colour beside it. The liver: taut inside and out, as though running along a knife's edge, as though pressing the string of a zither; blue and white without lustre, the hair breaking, then death. The heart: hard and beating, as though running along strung job's-tears seeds; red and black without lustre. The lung: large and empty, as a feather brushing the skin; white and red without lustre. The kidney: beating and cut off, as a finger flicking a stone; black and yellow without lustre. The spleen: weak and now quick now sparse; yellow and green without lustre. Of all five the chapter says that where the bare depot pulses appear, all die and are not to be treated. Before them it grades the whole case by term: the great bones withered, the great flesh sunken, the chest full and the breathing not free, and the qi moving the body, six months; with inward pain drawing to shoulder and neck, one month; with a hot body, the flesh gone and the muscle bulk broken, within ten months; with the marrow of the shoulder melting away and movement failing further, one year; and with the eye sockets sunken and the patient not seeing people, immediate death, while one who does see people dies at the time of the phase that overcomes him. Where the bare depot appears, the chapter says, the day may then be given."@en ;
    skos:note "The clause about giving the day is what makes this a prognostic system and not a list of grave signs. The interval comes from the general state and the bare depot pulse is what licenses naming a date, so the two findings do different work and the chapter keeps them apart."@en ;
    skos:note "The last of the graded cases carries its own escape. One who does not see people dies at once; one who does see people dies at the time of the overcoming phase, which is a term the chapter does not bound. A protocol would have to fix that before anything here could fail."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 19 (Yu ji zhen zang lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 6" .

pmc:WushiWuxu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The five repletions and the five depletions, and the two conditions of surviving them"@en, "Die fünf Füllen und die fünf Leeren und die zwei Bedingungen ihres Überlebens"@de ;
    skos:definition "The chapter states that the five repletions kill and the five depletions kill, and then names both. Repletion: the pulse full, the skin hot, the belly distended, front and back stopped, and the sight and mind clouded. Depletion: the pulse fine, the skin cold, the qi scant, purging and diarrhoea, and food and drink not going in. Asked why some nevertheless live, the answer names one condition for each. Where gruel enters the stomach and the pouring purge stops, the depleted one lives; where the body sweats and the bowels then move, the replete one lives. These, the chapter says, are the signs of it."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the one prognosis read in this corpus that names its own rescue condition in the same breath as the death sentence, and names it as an event rather than as a state. Both conditions are things that either happen or do not, and both are observable by anyone at the bedside without a pulse. That combination is rare enough here to be the reason for the node."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 19 (Yu ji zhen zang lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 6" .

pmc:PingrenJuegu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Seven days without food, and the reckoning behind the number"@en, "Sieben Tage ohne Nahrung, und die Rechnung hinter der Zahl"@de ;
    skos:definition "Asked why a person dies who does not eat for seven days, the chapter answers with a measurement and an arithmetic. It gives the dimensions and capacities of stomach, small intestine, returning intestine and wide intestine, and their combined length and combined capacity. In the healthy person, it says, the stomach is full when the intestines are empty and the intestines full when the stomach is empty, alternately empty and full, so that the qi may go up and down. Of what is held, two dou of grain and one dou five sheng of water stay in the tract; a healthy person voids twice a day, two and a half sheng each time, five sheng in a day; seven days at five sheng is three dou five sheng, and the retained food and water are gone. Therefore, the chapter concludes, a healthy person who does not eat or drink for seven days dies, because the essence-qi of food and water, the humours and the fluids are all exhausted."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because of the derivation and not the number. The seven days are not reported from a case; they are computed from a stated capacity and a stated daily rate, and both figures are given, so the claim can be attacked at either of them without touching the conclusion. Very little in this graph exposes its own arithmetic that way."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also the one claim of this corpus that is about any human being rather than about a patient, an operator or a practitioner, and therefore the one on which a protocol would not have to construct its own population."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 32 (Ping ren jue gu), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 6" .

pmc:ChimaiGuanTongzi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Red vessels crossing the pupil, counted for years"@en, "Rote Gefäße über der Pupille, nach Jahren gezählt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Asked how life and death are decided in the scrofulous cold-and-heat swellings, the chapter says to turn the eye back and look: where there are red vessels within it running from above down across the pupil, one vessel means death in a year, one vessel and a half a year and a half, two vessels two years, two and a half two and a half, three vessels three years. And where red vessels are seen that do not run down across the pupil, the case can be treated."@en ;
    skos:note "The last clause is the reason this is the most examinable claim read in this corpus. The prognosis names the finding under which it predicts nothing, which is what a claim must do to be able to fail; and the finding itself is visible on a living eye, countable, and prior to the outcome by a year or more. Almost nothing else here has all three."@en ;
    skos:note "The passage stands twice in the Lingshu, and the doublet is not verbatim. In the chapter on cold and heat the whole rule is given, including the clause about the vessels that do not cross; in the chapter on examining the forearm the same count is given as one item in a long list of skin and pulse signs, with the count intact and without the treatable case. What repeats is the number and what does not repeat is the exception, which is the half that carries the examinability."@en ;
    skos:note "The two places were read in two different editions, and the reason is a defect of the digitisation and not of the work. In the Sibu congkan text used here, the eleventh juan breaks off inside the chapter on examining the forearm and a block of commentary belonging to a Suwen chapter stands in its place; the passage is absent there. It was read instead in the Wenyuange Siku quanshu text of the Lingshu, and both citations say which."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing lingshu 70 (Han re), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 10", "Huangdi neijing lingshu 74 (Lun ji zhen chi), in Lingshu jing, Wenyuange Siku quanshu text, juan 11" .

#
# The seasonal mis-needlings, and the fourth set of intervals
#

pmc:SishiCini a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Twelve mis-needlings by season and depth, each with its outcome"@en, "Zwölf Fehlnadelungen nach Jahreszeit und Tiefe, jede mit ihrem Ausgang"@de ;
    skos:definition "The chapter first places the qi by season — in spring in the channels, in summer in the fine network vessels, in the long summer in the flesh, in autumn in the skin, in winter in bone and marrow — and then names, for each season, what follows from needling at the wrong depth. Spring, the network vessels: blood and qi overflow outward, the man is short of breath. Spring, the flesh: blood and qi turn back on themselves, the qi rises. Spring, sinew and bone: blood and qi settle inward, the belly distends. Summer, the channels: blood and qi are exhausted, the man is slack and weary. Summer, the flesh: blood and qi withdraw inward, the man is easily frightened. Summer, sinew and bone: blood and qi rise counter to their course, the man is easily angered. Autumn, the channels: blood and qi rise counter to their course, the man becomes forgetful. Autumn, the network vessels: the qi does not go outward, the man lies down and does not wish to move. Autumn, sinew and bone: blood and qi scatter inward, the man shivers. Winter, the channels: blood and qi are all lost, the eyes do not see clearly. Winter, the network vessels: the inner qi leaks outward, and there remains a great numbness. Winter, the flesh: the yang qi is cut off, the man becomes forgetful. All these, the chapter says, are the illnesses of great contrariety, and it adds that one who needles without knowing the seasons of the channels turns the with-the-grain into the against-the-grain."@en ;
    skos:note "Twelve clauses, each with a consequence stated in the same register as the prescription, and the whole indexed by a calendar. Beside the day-stem prohibitions of Lingshu 61 this is the corpus's second body of date-bound rules, and it is the more complete of the two, because these say what follows and those do not."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:Cizhen ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 64 (Si shi ci ni cong lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 18" .

#
# The incantation whose efficacy is dated, and the way that has no spirits
#

pmp:Zhuyou a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The incantation after the cause"@en, "Die Beschwörung nach der Ursache"@de ;
    skos:definition "The treatment the corpus ascribes to antiquity: shifting the essence and transforming the qi, for which, it says, the incantation after the cause was enough. It is named as an office elsewhere in the corpus, where the chapter on assigning duties by fitness gives the spitting on abscesses and the incanting of illnesses to the sharp-tongued and slighting."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 13 (Yi jing bian qi lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 4" .

pmc:ZhuyouGujinYi a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "It worked in antiquity and does not work now"@en, "Sie wirkte im Altertum und wirkt jetzt nicht mehr"@de ;
    skos:definition "The chapter opens by asking why the ancients treated illness by shifting the essence and transforming the qi, so that the incantation after the cause sufficed, while now drugs are used within and needle and stone without, and some are cured and some are not. The answer is a difference of age and not of technique. In high antiquity men lived among the birds and beasts, moved to keep off the cold and dwelt in the shade to keep off the heat, had within them no burden of longing and without them no obligation of office; in that untroubled age the evil could not enter deeply, so drugs could not treat within and needle and stone could not treat without, and shifting the essence and the incantation were enough. The present age is not so: cares run within, the toil of the body injures without, the compliance with the four seasons is lost and the fitness of cold and heat gone against, the thieving wind comes often and the empty evil morning and evening, reaching within to the depots and the marrow and injuring without the orifices and the flesh, so that a small illness must become grave and a great one must kill. Therefore, the chapter concludes, the incantation can no longer bring anything to an end."@en ;
    skos:note "The form of this claim is what the graph should hold it for. It neither asserts nor denies that the practice works; it dates the end of its working and gives a reason lying outside the practice, in the condition of the age. A statement of that shape covers both outcomes in advance: where the incantation fails it is confirmed, and where it succeeds the age was not yet so far gone."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also a transmission reporting a decline of a capacity from inside, which the graph carries in other traditions as a claim about lineages and initiations. Here the cause named is not a broken lineage but a changed way of living, which is at least the kind of antecedent a later reader could dispute with evidence."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 13 (Yi jing bian qi lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 4" .

pmp:BihuSaiyou a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Shut the door and the window, and ask him repeatedly"@en, "Tür und Fenster schließen und wiederholt fragen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The procedure the same chapter names as the whole of the art when asked what the one thing is to which treatment comes down. Shut the door and stop the window; bind yourself to the sick man; ask him repeatedly about his circumstances, and go with what he wants. He who obtains the spirit flourishes; he who loses it perishes."@en ;
    skos:note "Held as a practice because the chapter states it as an instruction with a setting, a repetition and a stopping rule, and because it is the corpus's own answer to the question of what decides. It is also the counterpart to the passage in which a patient's report of relief is refused as evidence: here the patient's account is what is sought, and there it is what is set aside. The corpus uses the sick man's word for the diagnosis and not for the outcome, and the graph keeps both."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 13 (Yi jing bian qi lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 4" .

pmc:DaoWuGuishen a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The way has no ghosts and no spirits"@en, "Der Weg hat keine Geister und keine Dämonen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Of the needling of the present age the chapter says that where the depleted are filled and the full are drained, that is what all the ordinary workmen know alike; but that to take heaven for law and earth for model, to move as the correspondence answers, so that harmonising with it is like an echo and following it like a shadow — the way has no ghosts and no spirits, it comes alone and goes alone. The same chapter names the five things that are to be hung out for all under heaven, of which the first is the ordering of the spirit, and instructs that in every true needling the spirit must first be ordered."@en ;
    skos:note "The sentence is a denial of the very thing the graph most often finds asserted: that in an operation of this kind something other than the operator acts. It is denied inside a corpus which elsewhere assigns the incanting of illnesses as an office and which holds a chapter dating the efficacy of that incantation to antiquity. The line does not therefore run between the two classics but between passages, and the dispute node beside this one carries all four places."@en ;
    skos:note "The spirit that must be ordered in the same chapter is not the spirit denied. What is set in order is the operator's own, and the corpus elsewhere describes it as a perception preceding the senses; what is refused is an agent standing outside the procedure. Keeping the two apart is the whole reading of the passage and is the reason it is entered rather than paraphrased."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 25 (Bao ming quan xing lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 8" .

pmc:SituatingDaoWuGuishenAgency a pm:Situating ;
    rdfs:label "The way without spirits is placed at own agency"@en, "Der Weg ohne Geister wird bei eigener Trägerschaft verortet"@de ;
    pm:situatedNode pmc:DaoWuGuishen ;
    pm:axisValue pm:ownAgency ;
    pm:situationGround "The source does not leave the question open and does not set the distinction aside: it takes the side within it. What is denied is that a ghost or a spirit is at work, and what is put in its place is a procedure that answers a correspondence, with the operator ordering his own spirit first. That is the criterion this value names, and it is asserted here rather than assumed."@en ;
    skos:note "The declining value was considered and does not fit. A tradition declines the distinction when it holds that acting and being acted through do not apply to what it teaches; this passage applies the distinction and rejects one of its terms, which is the opposite operation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 25 (Bao ming quan xing lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 8" .

#
# The Classic of Difficulties: the grades of knowing, and the ghost as a symptom
#

pmc:ShenShengGongQiao a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Spirit, sage, craftsman, adept: the four grades of diagnostic knowing"@en, "Geist, Weiser, Könner, Geschickter: die vier Stufen des diagnostischen Wissens"@de ;
    skos:definition "He who knows by looking is called spirit; he who knows by listening is called sage; he who knows by asking is called craftsman; he who knows by feeling the pulse is called adept. The text glosses each in turn: knowing by looking is seeing the five colours and knowing thereby which illness it is; knowing by listening is hearing the five tones and distinguishing the illness by them; knowing by asking is asking after the five flavours he desires and knowing thereby where the illness arose and where it lies; knowing by feeling the pulse is examining at the inch opening and seeing repletion and depletion, and thereby which depot or palace the illness is in. It closes by quoting the classic: to know it from without is called sage, to know it from within is called spirit."@en ;
    skos:note "The grading is the reason for the node and the gloss is the reason it does not decide what a reader might want it to decide. Read as a scale of capacity, the highest grade is a knowing by sight alone and bears the word spirit; read with the gloss the recension prints, the same grade is the reading of complexion, and the commentators supply worked instances — a green complexion in the liver sector, a red one meaning the heart is riding the liver. The recension read here therefore carries both the exalted term and a deflationary explanation of it, side by side, and the graph holds the passage without settling between them."@en ;
    skos:note "It stands in the graph beside the Suwen chapter in which the practitioner alone sees where all are looking and the mouth cannot say it. That passage refuses explanation and this one supplies it, in the same corpus and about the same faculty, which is a difference worth having on record before either is used for anything."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Nanjing 61, in Wang Hanlin jizhu Huangdi bashiyi nanjing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 4" .

pmc:TuoyangJiangui a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "He who has lost the yang sees ghosts"@en, "Wer das Yang verloren hat, sieht Geister"@de ;
    skos:definition "Doubled yang is mania; doubled yin is falling sickness; he who has lost the yang sees ghosts; he who has lost the yin goes blind. The commentary printed with the text ties each to a pulse: the commentator states that in loss of the yang there is no yang qi, that the pulse before the bar is extremely fine and faint, and that therefore the man sees wrongly within the eye and beholds ghostly things; and that in loss of the yin the depots no longer supply the eyes, so nothing is seen."@en ;
    skos:note "The graph carries a number of transmissions in which the seeing of beings is a capacity, acquired or given, and reported as an attainment. Here a canonical work of the same civilisation lists it among four disorders, between mania and blindness, and assigns it a pulse finding. That is a counter-attestation in the strict sense and not a difference of vocabulary: the same event, one register calling it an attainment and another a symptom."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Nanjing 20, in Wang Hanlin jizhu Huangdi bashiyi nanjing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 3" .

pmc:GuishenDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether spirits have anything to do with the operation"@en, "Ob Geister mit dem Verfahren etwas zu tun haben"@de ;
    skos:definition "Four places in one corpus, and they do not agree. The chapter on preserving life for the whole form says of the procedure that the way has no ghosts and no spirits, that it comes alone and goes alone. The Classic of Difficulties lists the seeing of ghosts among four disorders and assigns it a pulse. Against them, the chapter on shifting the essence has the incantation after the cause as the whole of the treatment of antiquity, and the chapter on assigning duties by fitness gives the incanting of illnesses and the spitting on abscesses as regular offices to be assigned by temperament. The corpus therefore carries an operative register with incantation in it and a register that denies that anything of the kind is at work, and neither passage mentions the other."@en ;
    skos:note "The dividing line is not between the two classics. Both the denial and the incantation stand in the Suwen, one in the chapter on shifting the essence and one in the chapter on preserving life, four juan apart. Whoever cites either side as the position of this corpus is choosing a passage."@en ;
    skos:note "The chapter that dates the incantation's efficacy to antiquity is on the side that admits it, and it is also the passage that makes the disagreement survivable: on its account the incantation once worked and no longer does, which is compatible with a present-day procedure that has no spirits in it. That is a reconciliation the corpus does not draw itself, and the graph does not draw it either. It is recorded here as what a later reader would have to argue for, not as what the sources say."@en ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmp:Zhuyou ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChineseMedicalCanon ;
    dcterms:source "Huangdi neijing suwen 25 (Bao ming quan xing lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 8", "Huangdi neijing suwen 13 (Yi jing bian qi lun), in Chongguang buzhu Huangdi neijing suwen, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 4", "Huangdi neijing lingshu 73 (Guan neng), in Huangdi suwen lingshu jing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 11", "Nanjing 20, in Wang Hanlin jizhu Huangdi bashiyi nanjing, Sibu congkan facsimile, juan 3" .
